<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of South Australia</h1><p class="st-meta">1836 CE – 1901 CE · capital Adelaide</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Australia's planned free colony, founded on Wakefield's theories of systematic settlement and later a pioneer of democratic reforms.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon-wakefield">Edward Gibbon Wakefield's</a> ideas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-australian-colonisation-commission">South Australian Colonisation Commission</a> sold land to fund free emigration, so that the new colony would receive no convicts. The first settlers arrived in 1836 and Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hindmarsh">John Hindmarsh</a> proclaimed the province at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holdfast-bay">Holdfast Bay</a> on 28 December. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-light">Colonel William Light</a> chose and laid out the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Early financial crisis forced Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grey">George Grey</a> to impose austerity in the 1840s, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> finds at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapunda">Kapunda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burra-south-australia">Burra</a> and wheat farming brought prosperity. Many Lutheran settlers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and religious dissenters gave the colony a reputation for tolerance. It introduced the secret ballot and adult male suffrage in 1856, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torrens-title">Torrens title</a> system of land registration in 1858, and votes for women in 1894.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>South <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Australia</a> also administered the Northern Territory from 1863 to 1911, building the Overland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Telegraph</a> from Adelaide to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darwin-northern-territory">Darwin</a> in 1872. It joined the federation and became a state of the Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 1901.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-south-australia.jpg" alt="Province of South Australia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Tsange · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Australia_(1876%E2%80%931904).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1836 CE – 1901 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Adelaide</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Hindmarsh, George Grey, Charles Kingston</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Methodism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Australia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of South Australia, South Australia</dd></dl></aside></div>
